Implementing a text editor with multiple lists (or more accurately, multi-selection) in Angular involves several steps. Below is an example of how you might structure such a component.

### Step 1: Define the Component

First, define your Angular component:

```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-text-editor',
  templateUrl: './text-editor.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./text-editor.component.css']
})
export class TextEditorComponent {
  // Your data models and services here
}
```

### Step 2: Create HTML Template

Next, create the template for your component using Angular's `*ngFor` directive to iterate over each selection:

```html
<!-- templates/text-editor.component.html -->
<div>
  <textarea #editor></textarea>
</div>

<div *ngIf="selectedItems$ | async as selectedItems">
  <ul>
    <li *ngFor="let item of selectedItems" (click)="removeItem(item)">
      {{item}}
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>
```

In this example:
- The `<textarea>` element serves as the main editing area.
- An array-like interface (`selectedItems$`) holds the currently selected items.
- Each item in the list is clickable; when clicked, it removes itself from the selection.

### Step 3: Update Data Binding

Ensure that your component updates the `selectedItems$` observable whenever changes occur in the textarea content. This can be done using Angular's `@Output` decorator or directly updating the property on the parent component if necessary.

### Step 4: Implement Selection Logic

To handle multi-selection logic, you can use Angular's `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` strategy. This ensures that the component detects changes immediately without needing to re-run its change detection.

```typescript
// src/app/text-editor.service.ts
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';
import { BehaviorSubject } from 'rxjs';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class TextEditorService {
  private selectedItems = new BehaviorSubject<string[]>([]);

  get selectedItems$: Observable<string[]> {
    return this.selectedItems.asObservable();
  }

  selectItem(item: string): void {
    const updatedList = [...this.selectedItems.value];
    updatedList.includes(item)
      ? this.selectedItems.next(updatedList.filter(x => x !== item))
      : this.selectedItems.next([...updatedList, item]);
  }
}
```

### Step 5: Use in Parent Component

Finally, inject the service into your parent component and subscribe to the observable to manage the state of selected items.

```typescript
// src/app/parent-component.component.ts
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { TextEditorService } from './text-editor.service';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-parent-component',
  templateUrl: './parent-component.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./parent-component.component.css']
})
export class ParentComponent {
  constructor(private textEditorService: TextEditorService) {}

  selectedItems: string[];

  ngOnInit() {
    this.textEditorService.selectedItems$.subscribe(items => {
      this.selectedItems = items;
    });
  }

  addNewItem(newItem: string) {
    this.textEditorService.selectItem(newItem);
  }

  removeSelectedItem(index: number) {
    this.textEditorService.selectItem(this.selectedItems[index]);
  }
}
```

### Step 6: Update View

Update the view to reflect the selected items dynamically based on user interaction:

